B. Why you used reserve Proxy here?
A Reverse Proxy sits between clients/users and backend servers.
It receives requests from users and forwards them to the appropriate backend servers.
In short, a reverse proxy is used to receive user requests and forward them to backend servers securely and efficiently. It also reuses existing connections to improve performance.
4. As you mentioned, we can use an ALB for application access and an NLB for databases. What is the difference between ALB and NLB?
ALB (Application Load Balancer) works on Layer 7 (Application Layer). It accepts HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket traffic and supports content-based routing, including path-based and host-based routing.
NLB (Network Load Balancer) works on Layer 4 (Transport Layer). It accepts TCP, UDP, and TLS traffic and supports IP- and port-based routing. 
It provides very high performance and low latency.
5. Why is NLB faster than ALB?
NLB is faster than ALB because it operates at Layer 4 and performs only TCP/UDP-level routing without inspecting application content. 
ALB works at Layer 7 and processes HTTP headers, URLs, and routing rules, which adds additional overhead and latency.
