 Terraform Workspaces: Managing Multiple Environments Efficiently
As infrastructure grows, managing separate environments (Dev, QA, UAT, Prod) becomes challenging.
One Terraform feature that simplifies this is Workspaces.
✅ What are Terraform Workspaces?
Workspaces allow you to manage multiple state files using the same Terraform configuration.
Example:
terraform workspace list
terraform workspace new dev
terraform workspace new prod
terraform workspace select prod
Benefits:
✔ Reuse the same code for multiple environments
✔ Maintain separate state files automatically
✔ Reduce code duplication
✔ Simplify environment management
Example Use Case:
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  instance_type = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? "t3.medium" : "t3.micro"
}
In this example:
Dev environment uses a smaller instance
Production uses a larger instance
Same Terraform codebase manages both
⚠️ Best Practice:
For enterprise environments, combine Terraform Workspaces with:
Remote State (S3)
State Locking (DynamoDB)
Separate CI/CD Pipelines
Environment-specific variables
Using Terraform Workspaces correctly helps maintain clean, scalable, and reusable Infrastructure as Code.

